{"content":"Fixed the regression: in sqlparse/engine/statement_splitter.py, the method _handle_nested_block had an inverted condition `if not (unified == 'WHILE'):` that fired for almost every keyword token, short-circuiting with `return 0` before the LOOP/DO and IF/CASE nested-block detection logic could run. This broke detection of FOR/WHILE/LOOP/IF/CASE blocks nested inside BEGIN...END, which cascaded into breaking DECLARE-block splitting (issue193, issue194) and CASE/WHEN splitting, among other related statement-splitting behaviors.

Fix applied: changed the condition to `if unified == 'WHILE':` so only WHILE keywords set up the unconfirmed-start, allowing other keywords to proceed to the LOOP/DO and IF/CASE checks as originally intended.

Verified manually with a CREATE PROCEDURE ... BEGIN DECLARE ... CASE WHEN ... END; END GO SELECT 1; script, which now correctly splits into two statements (the full procedure block through GO, then the trailing SELECT) instead of splitting incorrectly inside the block.","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_1","name":"finish","arguments":{"summary":"Fixed regression in sqlparse/engine/statement_splitter.py: _handle_nested_block had an inverted condition `if not (unified == 'WHILE'):` that incorrectly short-circuited with return 0 for nearly all keywords, preventing LOOP/DO and IF/CASE nested-block detection from running. Changed to `if unified == 'WHILE':`. This restores correct splitting behavior for DECLARE blocks (issue193, issue194), CASE/WHEN splitting, and other BEGIN...END nested control structure handling. Verified manually that a CREATE PROCEDURE with DECLARE, CASE/WHEN, END, GO now splits correctly into the expected two statements."}}]}
